Integrate and use the initial conditions.
$\begin{aligned}\underset{\sim}{v}(t)&=-4t\,\underset{\sim}{j}+\underset{\sim}{c}\\\underset{\sim}{c}&=6\underset{\sim}{i}+8\underset{\sim}{j}\\\underset{\sim}{v}(t)&=6\underset{\sim}{i}+(8-4t)\underset{\sim}{j}\\\underset{\sim}{r}(t)&=6t\,\underset{\sim}{i}+(8t-2t^{2})\underset{\sim}{j}+\underset{\sim}{d}\\\underset{\sim}{d}&=\underset{\sim}{0}\end{aligned}$
(a) Velocity is parallel to $\underset{\sim}{i}$ when its vertical component is zero; its horizontal component is the nonzero constant $6$.
$\begin{aligned}8-4t&=0\\t&=2\\\underset{\sim}{r}(2)&=12\underset{\sim}{i}+(16-8)\underset{\sim}{j}\\&=12\underset{\sim}{i}+8\underset{\sim}{j}\end{aligned}$
(b) Returning to $y=0$ requires the vertical position to be zero.
$\begin{aligned}8t-2t^{2}&=0\\2t(4-t)&=0\end{aligned}$
The roots are $t=0$ and $t=4$. The next return is at $t=4$.
$\begin{aligned}\underset{\sim}{r}(4)&=24\underset{\sim}{i}+(32-32)\underset{\sim}{j}\\&=24\underset{\sim}{i}\end{aligned}$
The diagram marks the two positions and the horizontal velocity at $t=2$.
